+- `PaddleOCR`, `OCRmyPDF`, and `docTR` are the strongest open source starting points for policy packets, loss runs, ACORD forms, and scanned claims files.
+- `Whisper`, `faster-whisper`, `pyannote-audio`, and `Vosk` cover call transcription, diarization, and voice analytics for sales and service workflows.
+- `RAGFlow`, `Open WebUI`, `Dify`, `Flowise`, and `Langflow` are the most useful repos for private knowledge assistants trained on underwriting guides, product manuals, and SOPs.
+- `n8n`, `Activepieces`, and `Automatisch` are the most practical workflow layers for intake, follow-up, enrichment, and handoff automations.
+- True insurance-agent-specific GitHub repos are still sparse, so this list prioritizes tools that are operationally relevant to insurance teams rather than pretending a mature niche category already exists.
+
+## Why This List
+Most “AI tools for insurance agents” lists mix generic SaaS products, affiliate links, and tools with no public code. This repository does the opposite: every entry is a public GitHub repository, relevant to a real insurance workflow, and suitable for teams that care about private data handling, customization, cost control, and long-term maintainability.
+
+Insurance agencies and brokerages deal with sensitive client records, scanned PDFs, recorded calls, policy comparisons, submissions, claims notes, and renewal reminders. Open source tools matter here because they make it easier to self-host, audit prompts and pipelines, integrate with existing systems, and avoid pushing regulated data into opaque black boxes.
+
